This poster studies how to make distributed dense QR run efficiently on modern GPU clusters. Dense QR factorization is a core primitive for least-squares solvers, orthogonalization, eigensolvers, and many machine learning workloads. On distributed GPU systems, however, existing libraries still leave a large fraction of hardware peak unused because panel factorization and its associated communication remain on the critical path. This poster presents a macro–micro co-designed distributed QR framework. At the macro level, our approach replaces the conventional 2D block-cyclic layout with a 1D column block-cyclic layout and removes idle bubbles through a critical-path-guided pipeline. At the micro level, it optimizes the local TSQR/WY path, reshapes trailing updates with two-level blocking, and reduces workspace pressure with chained GEMM updates. It reaches 86.3% of FP32 peak on a single A100, 86.0% on a single H200, and 75.5% of peak on 8 × H200 with explicit Q. More importantly, it raises 8-GPU H200 strong-scaling efficiency to 73% versus 46% for cuSOLVERMp, directly targeting the intra-node regime most relevant in practice. It also maintains a stable 1.6 × speedup on 16 × H200.